"Done. The `fmtcsv` project is set up in `/home/agent/workspace` and all tests pass.\n\n**Files created:**\n\n- **`fmtcsv.py`** — CLI tool with a `summarize(path)` function and a `main(argv)` entry point. It reads the CSV with Python's `csv` module (opened with `newline=\"\"` as required), so RFC 4180 quoting is handled correctly: quoted commas, escaped `\"\"` quotes, and embedded newlines all work, and leading/trailing spaces *inside* quotes are preserved exactly. Output prints `Total rows` (data rows after the header), `Total columns` (header field count), and `First data row`. Missing files exit with code 2 and an error on stderr.\n- **`test_fmtcsv.py`** — 10 pytest tests covering: simple CSV, quoted field with commas, spaces preserved inside quotes (e.g. `\"  padded  \"` stays intact), unquoted spaces kept verbatim, escaped quotes, newlines inside quoted fields, empty file, header-only file, CLI stdout output, and the missing-file error path.\n\n**Test run:** 10 passed in 0.02s\n\nOne environment note: system Python is PEP 668 externally-managed, so I created a local venv at `.venv` with pytest 9.1.1. Run the suite with:\n\n```\n.venv/bin/python -m pytest test_fmtcsv.py -v\n```\n\nAnd use the tool with:\n\n```\n.venv/bin/python fmtcsv.py file.csv\n```\n\nA verified end-to-end run:\n\n```\n$ .venv/bin/python fmtcsv.py demo.csv\nTotal rows: 2\nTotal columns: 3\nFirst data row: ['Alice', ' NYC ', 'Hello, world']\n```\n\nThat demo also surfaced one intentional behavior worth knowing: a space *before* an opening quote (` \"Hello, world\"`) is malformed per RFC 4180, so the quote is treated as a literal character rather than a delimiter — which is exactly what Python's default `csv` reader (and strict RFC behavior) does."